# Raw and derived data are reproducible from snapshots + code; snapshots are not.
data/raw/**
!data/raw/**/.gitkeep
data/processed/**
!data/processed/.gitkeep
*.duckdb

# Snapshots ARE the archival product (S84) - never ignore them.
!data/snapshots/**

.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
dist/

# The live draft board (S83) is committed WHOLE -- sheets and method artifacts
# both. The sheets have to be openable from a phone at a draft table with no
# checkout; the artifacts are the machine-readable half of the same deliverable,
# and S76's audit reads them after the fact.
#
# The market-dependent two used to be ignored here, on the argument that they are
# regenerated on every refresh and the snapshots behind them are archived anyway.
# Both halves of that are true and the conclusion was still wrong. `2026-draft` is
# regenerated IN PLACE, so the board a draft was actually made from survived
# nowhere once the runner was reclaimed -- and it cannot be rebuilt, because
# `survival.py` prices off `snapshot_date.max()` with no as-of pin, so a rebuild
# the morning after quotes prices the sheet never carried. A file that exists only
# inside a GitHub runner is not an archive.
#
# The cost is about 1.5 MB a day against the ~520 KB of sheets this same job
# already commits daily -- the same order, for the half that S76 cannot work
# without. The dated editions (artifacts/2026.08.13-r1/methods/) have always
# committed theirs; the live board was the sole exception, and the only edition
# anyone drafts from.
